1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,087
On this map, one division symbol becomes a corps symbol.

2
00:00:04,037 --> 00:00:06,562
What exactly has been added?

3
00:00:06,562 --> 00:00:09,650
The answer is not a fixed new head count.

4
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:13,950
The manual defines formations by roles and subordinate units.

5
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:19,237
Above the division, headquarters organise other headquarters and tailored formations.

6
00:00:19,237 --> 00:00:23,000
A neat four-at-every-step model stops matching doctrine.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,062
If the next symbol is a corps, what sits above it, and what does 'army' mean here?

8
00:00:28,062 --> 00:00:30,450
Keep that distinction open.

9
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:33,837
First overlay: the rule we should not extend.

10
00:00:33,837 --> 00:00:39,750
In our original diagram, one hypothetical leader grouped four at each step until a division.

11
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:48,250
That was a mathematical illustration, not a statement that real United States Army organizations always have four subordinate units at every echelon.

12
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:54,037
FM three dash zero describes corps headquarters and divisions in the Army's command structure.

13
00:00:54,037 --> 00:00:58,525
This source does not set a permanent number of divisions for every corps.

14
00:00:58,525 --> 00:01:03,662
We use its role descriptions rather than multiplying a hypothetical branch count.

15
00:01:03,662 --> 00:01:08,712
A corps marker alone does not determine exact personnel or subordinate totals.

16
00:01:08,712 --> 00:01:14,662
The Army's FM three dash zero describes relationships and responsibilities that vary with mission.

17
00:01:14,662 --> 00:01:19,725
Our branches are illustrations, not actual staff counts or an order of battle.

18
00:01:19,725 --> 00:01:27,950
FM three dash zero describes the division as a principal tactical formation that commands brigade combat teams and supporting organizations.

19
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:30,987
Its subordinate composition can vary.

20
00:01:30,987 --> 00:01:36,987
This example names only the functions supported by the cited doctrine, not a prescribed headcount.

21
00:01:36,987 --> 00:01:42,437
Here a corps headquarters connects to illustrative divisions and other attached formations.

22
00:01:42,437 --> 00:01:50,275
FM three dash zero describes how command echelons employ different subordinate organizations, but these links remain a teaching diagram.

23
00:01:50,275 --> 00:01:54,125
No depicted box identifies a real deployed unit.

24
00:01:54,125 --> 00:01:58,350
Imagine reusing our four-way branching model above the division.

25
00:01:58,350 --> 00:02:04,112
Four divisions under one box would be a hypothetical instance, not a doctrine requirement.

26
00:02:04,112 --> 00:02:07,075
We leave every force-strength cell blank.

27
00:02:07,075 --> 00:02:12,925
A beautiful multiplication cannot turn an illustrative branch into documented troop figures.

28
00:02:12,925 --> 00:02:20,412
Compare two columns: a mathematics column labelled if four branches, and a doctrine column labelled assigned or attached formations.

29
00:02:20,412 --> 00:02:27,925
The first specifies a numerical rule because we invented one; the second reports that actual composition is task-organised.

30
00:02:27,925 --> 00:02:31,962
We must not carry the invented rule into the official column.

31
00:02:31,962 --> 00:02:38,725
The marks on this overlay are episode labels, not verified reproductions from FM one dash zero two point two.

32
00:02:38,725 --> 00:02:42,587
They help our teaching diagram separate boxes and levels.

33
00:02:42,587 --> 00:02:47,700
Do not read them as authenticated military symbols or a personnel inventory.

34
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:52,575
Our second map overlay distinguishes a corps from a field army headquarters.

35
00:02:52,575 --> 00:03:01,150
FM three dash zero describes a field army that may command multiple corps when required, while a corps has its own tactical and operational roles.

36
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:05,475
We do not infer an automatic extra level in every command chain.

37
00:03:05,475 --> 00:03:13,400
FM three dash zero identifies corps as a versatile echelon above brigade, able to operate at tactical and operational levels.

38
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:17,125
A field army may command several corps when required.

39
00:03:17,125 --> 00:03:21,512
This slide does not claim any specific theater army organization.

40
00:03:21,512 --> 00:03:26,212
A field army is not automatically present in every command diagram.

41
00:03:26,212 --> 00:03:33,737
FM three dash zero presents it as an operational headquarters used when required, with a variable number of attached corps.

42
00:03:33,737 --> 00:03:38,950
The drawn boxes illustrate possible functions, not actual named formations.

43
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:45,987
FM three dash zero describes a field army as an operational headquarters that may direct several corps when required.

44
00:03:45,987 --> 00:03:53,525
The listed corps and supporting organizations are variable attachments, not a fixed organizational template or a mandated number.

45
00:03:53,525 --> 00:03:59,175
The comparison now shows different command purposes rather than a fixed multiplication ladder.

46
00:03:59,175 --> 00:04:06,612
A corps headquarters may function tactically under a land component, and a field army may coordinate multiple corps when required.

47
00:04:06,612 --> 00:04:11,287
Those roles are documented in FM three dash zero, twenty twenty two.

48
00:04:11,452 --> 00:04:18,877
The word army can refer to different contexts, so one label alone is not enough to identify the actual command relationship.

49
00:04:18,827 --> 00:04:25,289
FM three dash zero specifies roles for corps, divisions, and a conditional field army headquarters.

50
00:04:25,289 --> 00:04:28,814
We do not infer detailed theater-wide arrangements.

51
00:04:28,814 --> 00:04:32,902
No real-world order of battle is being built on this page.

52
00:04:32,902 --> 00:04:37,252
Boxes stand for command echelons and doctrinal relationships.

53
00:04:37,252 --> 00:04:43,214
We omit unit identifiers, deployment positions, combat tasks and operational routes.

54
00:04:43,214 --> 00:04:49,214
The lesson is about published organizational language, not about arranging forces for an operation.

55
00:04:49,214 --> 00:04:52,527
Third overlay: the price of the simple sum.

56
00:04:52,527 --> 00:04:58,177
The earlier four-to-one model was chosen because powers of four make an intuitive demonstration.

57
00:04:58,177 --> 00:05:04,264
Its usefulness ends where actual published definitions stop describing fixed multiplicative growth.

58
00:05:04,264 --> 00:05:09,189
The corps-to-army level is exactly where that distinction becomes unavoidable.

59
00:05:09,189 --> 00:05:12,539
Our teaching labels are local to this episode.

60
00:05:12,539 --> 00:05:21,439
Without a verified quotation and table number from the military-symbol publication, these marks cannot be presented as authoritative echelon amplifiers.

61
00:05:21,439 --> 00:05:25,427
The lesson concerns the limits of interpreting a diagram.

62
00:05:25,427 --> 00:05:30,389
FM three dash zero distinguishes the functional roles of corps and divisions.

63
00:05:30,389 --> 00:05:35,564
A division normally commands brigade combat teams and other enabling formations.

64
00:05:35,564 --> 00:05:41,364
This explains why a label does not yield an exact number of subordinate people or organizations.

65
00:05:41,364 --> 00:05:48,139
When required, FM three dash zero describes a field army providing command and control for multiple corps.

66
00:05:48,139 --> 00:05:52,727
Its composition can include several kinds of attached organizations.

67
00:05:52,727 --> 00:05:58,139
This is not a universal number of corps under every headquarters or a fixed hierarchy.

68
00:05:58,139 --> 00:06:06,439
FM three dash zero describes different roles for corps and divisions, including tactical headquarters functions in large-scale combat operations.

69
00:06:06,439 --> 00:06:12,764
This overlay distinguishes those documented roles from broader unspecified theater responsibilities.

70
00:06:12,764 --> 00:06:15,939
It is not a verified theater army chart.

71
00:06:15,939 --> 00:06:22,814
The map can show corps and field army as different types of Army headquarters, but one symbol is not a personnel total.

72
00:06:22,814 --> 00:06:28,377
FM three dash zero documents possible command roles, not permanent unit counts.

73
00:06:28,377 --> 00:06:31,964
The exact organization needs additional evidence.

74
00:06:31,964 --> 00:06:35,389
We now run a sensitivity thought experiment.

75
00:06:35,389 --> 00:06:42,114
If the illustrative branch factor were three or five instead of four, the mathematical counts would change dramatically.

76
00:06:42,114 --> 00:06:47,564
However, changing the invented factor would not revise a single doctrinal definition.

77
00:06:47,564 --> 00:06:52,777
This contrast is a test of our model, not evidence that the Army reorganised.

78
00:06:52,777 --> 00:06:58,839
A map needs a compact legend, but the marks used on this teaching overlay are episode labels only.

79
00:06:58,839 --> 00:07:04,689
We have not verified their detailed symbolism against the original military-symbol manual.

80
00:07:04,689 --> 00:07:08,539
No real operational unit or strength is identified.

81
00:07:08,539 --> 00:07:14,177
Finally, the 'corps' and 'army' terms do not justify assigning a soldier count in this episode.

82
00:07:14,177 --> 00:07:19,977
We have consulted the doctrinal definitions, not a dated strength table for a named real unit.

83
00:07:19,977 --> 00:07:24,214
One evidence column remains intentionally blank: personnel.

84
00:07:24,214 --> 00:07:28,564
That restraint is an accuracy feature, not a missing decoration.

85
00:07:28,564 --> 00:07:31,064
Return to the legend ledger.

86
00:07:31,064 --> 00:07:34,814
Our columns read item, value, type and source.

87
00:07:34,814 --> 00:07:39,464
The first item is symbol definition from the military symbol manual.

88
00:07:39,464 --> 00:07:44,552
The second is the role of an army headquarters from FM three dash ninety four.

89
00:07:44,552 --> 00:07:48,502
The third is the illustrative mathematical branch factor.

90
00:07:48,502 --> 00:07:56,714
The first ledger row names corps and division functions using FM three dash zero, twenty twenty two, sections two eighty seven through two eighty nine.

91
00:07:56,714 --> 00:08:01,702
This row is a doctrinal description, not a calculated number of troops.

92
00:08:01,702 --> 00:08:08,764
The field army ledger row comes from FM three dash zero, twenty twenty two, sections two eighty four and two eighty five.

93
00:08:08,764 --> 00:08:13,539
It may direct multiple corps when required and has variable attachments.

94
00:08:13,539 --> 00:08:17,702
This row records the role without claiming a permanent template.

95
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:24,847
The next ledger row records the corps as a headquarters with tactical and operational functions under FM three dash zero.

96
00:08:24,797 --> 00:08:30,935
It does not claim that one specified theater army always commands a fixed number of corps or divisions.

97
00:08:30,935 --> 00:08:37,985
Item four: if four hypothetical branches are repeated, the simple mathematical model generates a power of four.

98
00:08:37,985 --> 00:08:41,835
That statement is calculated within an invented model.

99
00:08:41,835 --> 00:08:46,372
It does not establish numbers of divisions in a corps or corps in an army.

100
00:08:46,372 --> 00:08:49,147
No derived head count is displayed.

101
00:08:49,147 --> 00:08:58,172
Three things could change a real command diagram without changing the mathematical rule we drew: the mission, the assigned or attached formations, and the headquarters role.

102
00:08:58,172 --> 00:09:02,610
These are exactly why doctrinal descriptions use functional language.

103
00:09:02,610 --> 00:09:06,297
A fixed branching factor cannot substitute for them.

104
00:09:06,297 --> 00:09:12,922
The map returns to its original division symbol, now beside a corps and two distinct Army headquarters roles.

105
00:09:12,922 --> 00:09:18,247
We can read the published hierarchy while keeping its conditional relationships intact.

106
00:09:18,247 --> 00:09:23,222
Nothing on the page pretends to know the actual strength of an unnamed formation.

107
00:09:23,222 --> 00:09:29,310
A corps is defined by the formations and roles it commands, not by multiplying a division's head count.

108
00:09:29,310 --> 00:09:33,535
Above it, Army headquarters have distinct responsibilities.

109
00:09:33,535 --> 00:09:38,247
Read the echelon and function first; never invent the personnel total.

